  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nOverall synoptic pattern continues to be dominated by west-\nnorthwest flow aloft through through Wednesday, with periodic \nshortwave disturbances expected to bring shwrs/storms to \nportions of the Mid-Atlantic and Ohio Valley regions. While our\narea should largely remain dry, cannot rule out a passing shower\nor two late this afternoon and again overnight across the lower\nHudson Valley as cold front that's currently working through our\narea stalls south of our region. \n\nMeanwhile, best chance for organized rainfall across our entire\narea will arrive Thursday and Thursday night as a large-scale \ntrough begins moving through the Northeast. Deterministic models\nhave been fairly consistent on this in recent days, and NBM \npops continue to increase as a result. Instability will be \navailable for some thunderstorm activity, and strong wind \nfields aloft may bring the risk of severe storms to the area. \nTrends will have to be monitored. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA strong cold front associated with Thursday's upper trough will\nsweep across the region late Thursday and early Friday. Behind\nthis front, 850-hPa temps fall to 7 to 8C, which should yield\nhigh temps only in the middle to upper 70s by Saturday.\nTemperatures will begin to moderate by early next week as high\npressure slides east and southerly flow returns.",
